  published_at: string | null;
  created_at: string;
}
export type NewArticle = Omit<Article, "id" | "created_at">;
```

Goal: a dependency-free RSS ingest module. It must:
1. Fetch an RSS 2.0 feed over HTTP using the global `fetch` (Node 18+/Next runtime has it). Default feed URL: "https://rss.politico.com/congress.xml" but make it a constant array `FEED_URLS: string[]` so more feeds can be added later, and loop over all of them.
2. Parse the XML WITHOUT any external library. Write a small helper that extracts each `<item>...</item>` block via regex, then within each item pulls out `<title>`, `<link>`, `<description>`, and `<pubDate>`. Handle CDATA sections (`<![CDATA[ ... ]]>`) and decode basic HTML entities (&amp; &lt; &gt; &quot; &#39; &apos;). Strip HTML tags from the description to produce a plain-text snippet, and truncate the snippet to 500 chars.
3. Map each parsed item to a NewArticle: url=link (required — skip items with no link), title (required — skip items with no title), snippet=cleaned description or null, source = derived from the feed (e.g. hostname of the feed URL), published_at = raw pubDate string or null.
4. De-duplicate within the batch by url before upserting.
5. Upsert into the Supabase table "articles" using `.upsert(rows, { onConflict: "url", ignoreDuplicates: false })`. The table has a unique constraint on url. Throw on supabase error.
6. Export an async function `ingestArticles(): Promise<number>` that does all of the above across all FEED_URLS and returns the number of articles stored/updated. It should be resilient: if one feed fails to fetch/parse, log a warning (console.warn) and continue with the others.
7. At the bottom, add a CLI entrypoint guarded so it only runs when executed directly, not when imported:
```
// Allow running directly: `npx tsx lib/ingest.ts` or via a wrapper script.
if (process.argv[1] && /ingest\.ts$/.test(process.argv[1])) {
  ingestArticles()
    .then((n) => {
      console.log(`Stored/updated ${n} articles.`);
      process.exit(0);
    })
    .catch((err) => {
      console.error("Ingest failed:", err);
      process.exit(1);
    });
}
```

Code requirements: TypeScript strict mode, no `any` (use `unknown` + narrowing or precise types), clear JSDoc comments on exported functions, well-structured small helper functions (e.g. extractItems, extractTag, stripHtml, decodeEntities, cleanSnippet). Match the concise commenting style used in lib/politicians.ts and lib/supabase/server.ts.

